## Introduction

Clean code is code that is easy to read, understand, and maintain. Following clean code principles makes software more robust, reduces bugs, and improves team productivity.

## Meaningful Names

Use descriptive, intention-revealing names:

```java
// Bad
int d; // elapsed time in days
String s;
List<int[]> list1;

// Good
int elapsedTimeInDays;
String customerName;
List<Customer> activeCustomers;

// Class names: nouns
class Customer { }
class Account { }
class OrderProcessor { }

// Method names: verbs
void saveCustomer() { }
boolean isValid() { }
String getName() { }

// Avoid encodings
String nameString; // Bad
String name;       // Good

ICustomer customer; // Bad (Hungarian notation)
Customer customer;  // Good
```

## Functions Should Be Small

Keep functions focused:

```java
// Bad: Function does too much
void processOrder(Order order) {
    // Validate
    if (order.getItems().isEmpty()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Empty order");
    }

    // Calculate total
    double total = 0;
    for (Item item : order.getItems()) {
        total += item.getPrice() * item.getQuantity();
    }

    // Apply discount
    if (order.getCustomer().isPremium()) {
        total *= 0.9;
    }

    // Save to database
    database.save(order);

    // Send email
    emailService.send(order.getCustomer().getEmail(), "Order confirmed");
}

// Good: Separate concerns
void processOrder(Order order) {
    validateOrder(order);
    double total = calculateTotal(order);
    applyDiscounts(order, total);
    saveOrder(order);
    sendConfirmationEmail(order);
}

private void validateOrder(Order order) {
    if (order.getItems().isEmpty()) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Empty order");
    }
}

private double calculateTotal(Order order) {
    return order.getItems().stream()
        .mapToDouble(item -> item.getPrice() * item.getQuantity())
        .sum();
}
```

## Do One Thing

Single Responsibility Principle:

```python
# Bad: Multiple responsibilities
class UserManager:
    def create_user(self, data):
        # Validate data
        if not data.get('email'):
            raise ValueError("Email required")

        # Hash password
        hashed = bcrypt.hashpw(data['password'].encode(), bcrypt.gensalt())

        # Save to database
        user = User(email=data['email'], password=hashed)
        db.session.add(user)
        db.session.commit()

        # Send email
        send_email(data['email'], "Welcome!")

        # Log event
        logger.info(f"User created: {data['email']}")

# Good: Separate responsibilities
class UserValidator:
    def validate(self, data):
        if not data.get('email'):
            raise ValueError("Email required")

class PasswordHasher:
    def hash(self, password):
        return bcrypt.hashpw(password.encode(), bcrypt.gensalt())

class UserRepository:
    def save(self, user):
        db.session.add(user)
        db.session.commit()

class UserNotifier:
    def send_welcome_email(self, email):
        send_email(email, "Welcome!")

class UserService:
    def __init__(self, validator, hasher, repository, notifier):
        self.validator = validator
        self.hasher = hasher
        self.repository = repository
        self.notifier = notifier

    def create_user(self, data):
        self.validator.validate(data)
        hashed_password = self.hasher.hash(data['password'])
        user = User(email=data['email'], password=hashed_password)
        self.repository.save(user)
        self.notifier.send_welcome_email(data['email'])
        return user
```

## Comments: Explain Why, Not What

Code should be self-explanatory:

```java
// Bad: Redundant comment
// Increment i
i++;

// Bad: Commenting bad code
// Check if customer is eligible for discount
if (c.getAge() > 65 && c.getOrderCount() > 10 && !c.isBlocked()) {
    // Apply discount
}

// Good: Self-documenting code
if (isEligibleForSeniorDiscount(customer)) {
    applyDiscount(customer);
}

boolean isEligibleForSeniorDiscount(Customer customer) {
    return customer.getAge() > 65 
        && customer.getOrderCount() > 10
        && !customer.isBlocked();
}

// Good: Explain why
// We use a LinkedHashMap to preserve insertion order
// because the UI displays items in the order they were added
Map<String, Item> items = new LinkedHashMap<>();

// Good: Warning of consequences
// Don't run this in production! It will delete all user data.
void resetDatabase() { }
```

## Error Handling

Use exceptions, not error codes:

```java
// Bad: Error codes
int deleteUser(Long userId) {
    User user = userRepository.findById(userId);
    if (user == null) {
        return -1; // Not found
    }
    if (!hasPermission()) {
        return -2; // No permission
    }
    userRepository.delete(user);
    return 0; // Success
}

// Good: Exceptions
void deleteUser(Long userId) {
    User user = userRepository.findById(userId)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(userId));

    if (!hasPermission()) {
        throw new InsufficientPermissionException();
    }

    userRepository.delete(user);
}

// Don't return null
// Bad
List<Customer> getCustomers() {
    if (noCustomers) {
        return null; // Caller must check for null
    }
    return customers;
}

// Good
List<Customer> getCustomers() {
    if (noCustomers) {
        return Collections.emptyList();
    }
    return customers;
}
```

## DRY: Don't Repeat Yourself

Avoid code duplication:

```python
# Bad: Duplicated logic
def calculate_employee_salary(employee):
    base = employee.base_salary
    bonus = base * 0.1
    tax = (base + bonus) * 0.3
    return base + bonus - tax

def calculate_contractor_payment(contractor):
    base = contractor.hourly_rate * contractor.hours
    bonus = base * 0.1
    tax = (base + bonus) * 0.3
    return base + bonus - tax

# Good: Extract common logic
def calculate_payment(base_amount):
    bonus = base_amount * 0.1
    tax = (base_amount + bonus) * 0.3
    return base_amount + bonus - tax

def calculate_employee_salary(employee):
    return calculate_payment(employee.base_salary)

def calculate_contractor_payment(contractor):
    base = contractor.hourly_rate * contractor.hours
    return calculate_payment(base)
```

## KISS: Keep It Simple

Simplicity over cleverness:

```javascript
// Bad: Overly clever
const isEven = n => !(n & 1);

// Good: Clear and simple
const isEven = n => n % 2 === 0;

// Bad: Unnecessary abstraction
class StringManipulator {
  constructor(str) {
    this.str = str;
  }

  toUpperCase() {
    return new StringManipulator(this.str.toUpperCase());
  }

  toLowerCase() {
    return new StringManipulator(this.str.toLowerCase());
  }
}

// Good: Use built-in methods
const upper = str.toUpperCase();
const lower = str.toLowerCase();
```

## Formatting

Consistent style matters:

```java
// Bad: Inconsistent formatting
public class User{
private String name;private int age;
public String getName(){return name;}
public void setName(String name){this.name=name;}}

// Good: Consistent formatting
public class User {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}

// Vertical ordering: high-level to low-level
public class OrderProcessor {
    public void processOrder(Order order) {
        validateOrder(order);
        calculateTotal(order);
        saveOrder(order);
    }

    private void validateOrder(Order order) { }
    private void calculateTotal(Order order) { }
    private void saveOrder(Order order) { }
}
```

## Unit Tests

Clean tests are as important as clean code:

```java
// Bad: Multiple assertions, unclear
@Test
void testUser() {
    User user = new User("John", 30);
    assertEquals("John", user.getName());
    assertEquals(30, user.getAge());
    user.setName("Jane");
    assertEquals("Jane", user.getName());
}

// Good: One concept per test, clear names
@Test
void shouldReturnNameWhenUserIsCreated() {
    User user = new User("John", 30);
    assertEquals("John", user.getName());
}

@Test
void shouldReturnAgeWhenUserIsCreated() {
    User user = new User("John", 30);
    assertEquals(30, user.getAge());
}

@Test
void shouldUpdateNameWhenSetNameIsCalled() {
    User user = new User("John", 30);
    user.setName("Jane");
    assertEquals("Jane", user.getName());
}
```

## Refactoring

Continuously improve code:

```
1. Red: Write failing test
2. Green: Make test pass (quick & dirty)
3. Refactor: Clean up code

Refactoring techniques:
- Extract Method
- Rename Variable/Method
- Replace Magic Numbers with Constants
- Extract Class
- Replace Conditional with Polymorphism
```

## Code Review Checklist

```
 Meaningful names
- Functions are small and focused
- No code duplication
- Proper error handling
- Tests are comprehensive
- No commented-out code
- Consistent formatting
- No magic numbers
- Clear separation of concerns
- Documentation where needed
```

## Conclusion

Clean code is not about perfectionâ€”it's about constant improvement. By following these principles, you write code that's easier to understand, test, and maintain. Remember: code is read far more often than it's written, so optimize for readability.
